<BaseLayout title="Methodology" description="The formulas, quota-inference methods, conservative range rules, and evidence classes behind Tokenmaxx monthly coding-subscription comparisons.">
  <section class="page-intro"><p class="eyebrow">Simple arithmetic, aggressive evidence collection</p><h1>A plan is useful only when its model, allowance, and quality evidence line up.</h1><p class="lede">Tokenmaxx searches official rate cards, authenticated usage APIs, local token logs, plan-labeled meter deltas, and quantified user measurements before declaring a quota unavailable.</p></section>
  <article class="prose section compact">
    <h2>Primary token calculation</h2>
    <pre class="formula"><code>{`blended API $/1M = fresh_share × fresh_input_rate
                    + cache_share × cache_read_rate
                    + output_share × output_rate

included raw tokens (M) = model-eligible monthly pool / blended API $/1M
subscription $/1M       = monthly subscription price / included raw tokens (M)`}</code></pre>
    <p>The standardized profile uses 20% fresh input, 70% cache reads, and 10% output. The agentic profile uses 2.594% fresh input, 97.108% cache reads, and 0.298% output from public coding-agent telemetry.</p>

    <h2>Quota inversion</h2>
    <pre class="formula"><code>{`full bucket units ≈ normalized local usage delta / displayed quota-fraction delta

full bucket API value ≈ summed token-category cost / displayed quota-fraction delta`}</code></pre>
    <p>This is the same method used for the hidden Codex pool. A measurement is accepted only when its plan, time window, reset state, model or route, local usage, and displayed meter movement are identifiable. A shared-product meter such as Grok cannot be assigned to one paid tier from an unlabeled account sample.</p>

    <h2>Ranges and lower bounds</h2>
    <p>{guide.methodology.rangeRankingRule || 'Measured ranges use their lower capacity bound in rankings.'}</p>
    <pre class="formula"><code>{`conservative subscription $/1M = subscription price / lower capacity bound

lower-bound plan cost is an upper bound:
true capacity ≥ observed capacity  ⇒  true $/1M ≤ displayed $/1M`}</code></pre>
    <p>Midpoint and upper estimates remain in the route data and on plan pages. Historical quota regimes are stored separately and do not silently inflate a current estimate.</p>

    <h2>Quality-adjusted token cost</h2><pre class="formula"><code>{`quality-adjusted subscription $/1M
  = conservative subscription $/1M / (external intelligence score / 100)`}</code></pre><p>This makes a billion tokens of a weak model worth less than a billion tokens of a stronger model while preserving the original price and score. It is a density metric, not a claim that intelligence is linear.</p>

    <h2>Task economics</h2><pre class="formula"><code>{`external API $/successful task = published API $/attempt / published pass rate
monthly task attempts           = conservative included raw tokens / published raw tokens per task
monthly successful tasks        = attempts × published pass rate
subscription $/successful task  = subscription price / successful tasks`}</code></pre><p>Task results remain tied to the external benchmark and harness. They are plan-capacity counterfactuals, not promises that another agent harness reproduces the same pass rate.</p>

    <h2>Six comparison classes</h2><ul><li><strong>Token:</strong> an exact, measured-range, or lower-bound model-token denominator.</li><li><strong>Request:</strong> numeric request weights and counts, but no stable tokens per request.</li><li><strong>Managed:</strong> platform credits, agent work units, app-builder tokens, daily tasks, processing tokens, or fair-use throughput.</li><li><strong>Relative:</strong> a multiplier is published, but the absolute base pool or model weights are not.</li><li><strong>Calibratable:</strong> the provider’s own meter and local logs expose a reproducible denominator, but the available public sample is not sufficient to assign one universal plan value.</li><li><strong>Provider-hidden:</strong> neither the provider nor a defensible measurement exposes the allowance or deduction fields required for the requested metric.</li></ul>

    <h2>Provider-specific examples</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Cursor:</strong> exact $20/$70/$400 Other Models pools; separate numerical Cursor Models pool remains unknown.</li>
      <li><strong>Claude Code:</strong> current raw-token and API-equivalent ranges reconstructed from local logs and weekly-meter movement.</li>
      <li><strong>Kimi Code:</strong> Allegretto longitudinal measurement scaled by the official plan relationship, with K3 1M and HighSpeed quota weights applied.</li>
      <li><strong>Factory:</strong> Standard-token pools and model multipliers converted through cache/fresh/output weights.</li>
      <li><strong>Ollama:</strong> conservative raw-token lower bound from a public Pro run plus the official Max-to-Pro multiplier.</li>
      <li><strong>Antigravity:</strong> regime-adjusted mixed-route range after Google’s documented paid-plan quota increases.</li>
      <li><strong>Grok and Cline:</strong> authenticated usage endpoints plus local transaction/token logs provide an account-specific calibration path.</li>
    </ul>
    <p><a href="https://github.com/siraht/TokenmaxxDash/blob/main/docs/QUOTA_INFERENCE.md" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Read the complete provider derivations in the repository ↗</a></p>

    <h2>Owned-plan behavior</h2><p>{guide.methodology.ownedPlanRule}</p>
    <h2>Missing data</h2><p>{guide.methodology.missingDataRule}</p><p>A provider-hidden record is a completed research result only after official, API, local-log, and quantified community evidence paths have been checked. It remains in the catalog with the exact missing fields and is excluded only from rankings that require that denominator.</p>
    <h2>Current model access exceptions</h2><p>Claude Fable 5 is PAYG-only on Claude Pro. Claude Max includes Fable but caps it at 50% of the shared weekly meter. Kimi K3 1M consumes approximately twice K3 256K quota, while Kimi HighSpeed consumes approximately three times Standard quota. Cursor’s first-party Grok and Composer models use a different pool from its exact Other Models dollar allowance.</p>
  </article>
